 # JAVASCRIPT OBJECTS - COMPLETE NOTES

## 1. KEY-VALUE STRUCTURE

An object stores data in key-value pairs.

let user = {
    name: "Mayank",
    age: 22,
    city: "Aligarh",
    isStudent: true
};

console.log(user);
console.log(user.name);
console.log(user.age);

Adding, updating and deleting:

user.country = "India";  // Add
user.age = 23;           // Update
delete user.city;        // Delete

## 2. DOT NOTATION AND BRACKET NOTATION

A. Dot notation:

let student = {
    name: "Rahul",
    age: 21
};

console.log(student.name);
console.log(student.age);

B. Bracket notation:

let student = {
    name: "Rahul",
    "full address": "Aligarh UP"
};

console.log(student["name"]);
console.log(student["full address"]);

Dynamic property access:

let key = "name";

let user = {
    name: "Mayank",
    age: 22
};

console.log(user.key);   // undefined
console.log(user[key]);  // Mayank

## 3. NESTING AND DEEP ACCESS

An object inside another object is called a nested object.

let user = {
    name: "Mayank",
    age: 22,
    address: {
        city: "Aligarh",
        state: "Uttar Pradesh",
        pincode: {
            area: 202001
        }
    }
};

console.log(user.name);
console.log(user.address.city);
console.log(user.address.state);
console.log(user.address.pincode.area);

Array inside an object:

let student = {
    name: "Mayank",
    skills: ["HTML", "CSS", "JavaScript"]
};

console.log(student.skills[0]);
console.log(student.skills[2]);

Object inside an array:

let students = [
    { name: "Mayank", age: 22 },
    { name: "Rahul", age: 21 }
];

console.log(students[0].name);
console.log(students[1].age);

## 4. OBJECT DESTRUCTURING

Destructuring extracts properties into variables.

Without destructuring:

let user = {
    name: "Mayank",
    age: 22,
    city: "Aligarh"
};

let name = user.name;
let age = user.age;

With destructuring:

let { name, age, city } = user;

console.log(name);
console.log(age);
console.log(city);

Renaming variables:

let { name: userName, age: userAge } = user;

console.log(userName);
console.log(userAge);

Default values:

let { name, age = 18 } = user;

Nested destructuring:

let person = {
    name: "Mayank",
    address: {
        city: "Aligarh",
        state: "UP"
    }
};

let {
    address: { city, state }
} = person;

console.log(city);
console.log(state);

## 5. LOOPING THROUGH OBJECTS

Example:

let user = {
    name: "Mayank",
    age: 22,
    city: "Aligarh"
};

A. for...in:

for (let key in user) {
    console.log(key);
    console.log(user[key]);
}

B. Object.keys():

let keys = Object.keys(user);
console.log(keys);

Object.keys(user).forEach(function(key) {
    console.log(key);
});

C. Object.values():

let values = Object.values(user);
console.log(values);

D. Object.entries():

let entries = Object.entries(user);
console.log(entries);

Using for...of:

for (let [key, value] of Object.entries(user)) {
    console.log(key, value);
}

Quick comparison:
- for...in: Iterates over enumerable property keys.
- Object.keys(): Returns an array of own enumerable string keys.
- Object.values(): Returns an array of values.
- Object.entries(): Returns an array of key-value pairs.

## 6. COPYING OBJECTS

A. Reference assignment:

let original = {
    name: "Mayank",
    age: 22
};

let copy = original;

copy.age = 25;

console.log(original.age); // 25
console.log(copy.age);     // 25

Both variables reference the same object.

B. Spread operator:

let original = {
    name: "Mayank",
    age: 22
};

let copy = { ...original };

copy.age = 25;

console.log(original.age); // 22
console.log(copy.age);     // 25

C. Object.assign():

let original = {
    name: "Mayank",
    age: 22
};

let copy = Object.assign({}, original);

copy.name = "Rahul";

console.log(original.name); // Mayank
console.log(copy.name);     // Rahul

D. Shallow copy:

let original = {
    name: "Mayank",
    address: {
        city: "Aligarh"
    }
};

let copy = { ...original };

copy.address.city = "Delhi";

console.log(original.address.city); // Delhi

The nested address object is shared.

E. Deep copy:

let original = {
    name: "Mayank",
    address: {
        city: "Aligarh"
    }
};

let copy = structuredClone(original);

copy.address.city = "Delhi";

console.log(original.address.city); // Aligarh
console.log(copy.address.city);     // Delhi

structuredClone() supports many data types but cannot clone functions.

## 7. OPTIONAL CHAINING (?.)

Safely accesses nested properties when an intermediate value might be null or undefined.

Without optional chaining:

let user = {};

console.log(user.address.city); // TypeError

With optional chaining:

console.log(user.address?.city); // undefined

Nested optional chaining:

let user = {
    profile: {
        address: {
            city: "Aligarh"
        }
    }
};

console.log(user.profile?.address?.city);

With arrays:

let user = {
    skills: ["JavaScript", "SQL"]
};

console.log(user.skills?.[0]);
console.log(user.skills?.[5]);

With functions:

let user = {};

user.greet?.();

Optional chaining does not protect undeclared variables.

## 8. COMPUTED PROPERTIES

Creates object keys dynamically using variables or expressions.

Without computed properties:

let key = "name";

let user = {
    key: "Mayank"
};

console.log(user.key);

With computed properties:

let key = "name";

let user = {
    [key]: "Mayank"
};

console.log(user.name);

Dynamic properties:

let property = "age";
let value = 22;

let user = {
    [property]: value
};

console.log(user);

Using expressions:

let first = "user";
let second = "name";

let obj = {
    [first + second]: "Mayank"
};

console.log(obj.username);

## 9. BONUS OBJECT CONCEPTS

A. Object.freeze():

Prevents adding, deleting or changing top-level properties.

let user = {
    name: "Mayank",
    age: 22
};

Object.freeze(user);

user.age = 25;
user.city = "Delhi";

console.log(user);

Note: Freeze is shallow.

B. Object.seal():

Prevents adding or deleting properties, but existing writable properties can be changed.

let user = {
    name: "Mayank",
    age: 22
};

Object.seal(user);

user.age = 25;
user.city = "Delhi";
delete user.name;

console.log(user);

C. Object.hasOwn():

Checks whether an object has its own property.

let user = {
    name: "Mayank",
    age: 22
};

console.log(Object.hasOwn(user, "name")); // true
console.log(Object.hasOwn(user, "city")); // false

D. this keyword:

let user = {
    name: "Mayank",

    greet: function() {
        console.log("Hello " + this.name);
    }
};

user.greet();

Output: Hello Mayank

## 10. QUICK REVISION CHEAT SHEET

Create object: { name: "Mayank" }
Dot notation: user.name
Bracket notation: user["name"]
Add property: user.city = "Delhi"
Delete property: delete user.city
Destructuring: let {name} = user
For-in: for (let key in user)
Object keys: Object.keys(user)
Object values: Object.values(user)
Object entries: Object.entries(user)
Spread copy: {...user}
Assign copy: Object.assign({}, user)
Deep clone: structuredClone(user)
Optional chaining: user.address?.city
Computed property: {[key]: value}
Freeze: Object.freeze(user)
Seal: Object.seal(user)
Own property: Object.hasOwn(user, "name")

## 11. PRACTICE QUESTIONS

1. Create an object with name, price, and inStock properties. Print name and price.

2. Access an object's age property using bracket notation and a variable called key.

3. Update a user's city from Aligarh to Delhi and add a country property.

4. Create a nested student object with address, city and state. Print the state.

5. Extract name and age from an object using destructuring.

6. Use for...in to print all keys and values of an object.

7. Use Object.entries() and for...of to print each key-value pair.

8. Create a shallow copy using spread syntax. Change the copied object's top-level property.

9. Deep clone a nested object using structuredClone() and modify its copied city.

10. Safely access user.profile.email using optional chaining.

11. Create an object property dynamically using a variable called key with the value "score".

12. Create a student object with name, marks and address. Destructure the name, loop through properties, deep clone the object and safely access address.city.